# Pantheon's plan, in John Carmack's .plan format (docs/PLAN.md): one item
# per line, under the day it was written. An unmarked line is open; "* " marks
# one done that day, "+ " one done later, "- " one dropped. The order is the
# priority. An item is a pointer: its details are in the files it names.

2026-11-08
* C1, C2, H3 and H7 fixed, each with a test (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW.md)
* a second, independent security review (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md)

2026-11-09
+ P-2: the node's git runs a clean filter a model writes into PANTHEON_GITDIR's config when node and model are one user: read the checkout without running anything its config names (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-2; shell.ml edits_of_checkout; a test/edits.sh case with the hostile config in PANTHEON_GITDIR itself)
+ sandbox fetch publishes the market's merges to each local checkout's default branch, not pantheon/merged: fast-forward, merge as publish.sh does, leave a dirty or conflicting checkout alone and say so (scripts/sandbox.sh cmd_fetch, scripts/publish.sh, docs/SANDBOX.md; a test/publish.sh case against a non-bare checkout)
P-10: make the node's own path absolute at startup, so `./main.exe` works (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-10)
P-9: genesis requires exactly four parts, with unique names and unique keys (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-9)
P-17: refuse NUL and control characters in specs, notes and reasons; give git the merge commit's message on stdin (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-17)
P-14: deny .gitattributes and .gitmodules in any path component, in any case (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-14)
P-16: bound a model's JSON reply before parsing it: depth, field count, iterative flattening (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-16)
P-6: decide that CI couldn't run from the node's own signals, never from the check's output (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-6)
P-5: keep future-nonce transactions from filling the pool (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-5)
P-8: make a leader-draw reveal unique, and prove two accepted reveals for one draw are equal (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-8; market.soma reaches)
P-4: keep consensus locks across a restart, with an e2e scenario restarting a locked node (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-4)
S-4 and P-7: CI refuses a bid that adds an unproved assumption (Admitted, an axiom, unk) or weakens the test it runs (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md S-4, P-7; the CI commands in scripts/sandbox.sh and scripts/host.sh)
docs: SAFETY.md and README.md say which setup each claim holds in, and PROOFS.md lists every proved theorem (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-1, P-12, "Proofs and claims")
